<h2>Two decades of litigation in one paragraph</h2>
<p>The parties married on 14 May 2012; a son was born in September 2013. The husband&rsquo;s divorce suit on mental cruelty was dismissed by the trial court in February 2017, and his matrimonial appeal failed in July 2018. In 2019 he returned with a petition for judicial separation under Section 10 HMA, pleading cruelty and desertion. The Family Court at Nalbari dismissed it in March 2021. The Division Bench — Justice Michael Zothankhuma and Justice Sanjeev Kumar Sharma — heard the resulting appeal and reserved judgment on 3 November 2026, pronouncing on 12 November 2026. In the background sat a residence and compensation order obtained by the wife under the DV Act.</p>

<h2>The Bench&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Findings bind.</strong> The findings recorded in the divorce suit, affirmed on appeal, bound the parties. A judicial separation petition between the same spouses, on the same statutory grounds, cannot reopen what those proceedings decided.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Pleadings must be anchored in time.</strong> Of the 29 grounds pleaded, all but one did not disclose whether the alleged events preceded or followed the divorce proceedings. Undated allegations collapse into the earlier record — and stand barred on principles of constructive res judicata.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The lone fresh ground failed on particulars.</strong> The only post-dismissal allegation — that the wife had become &ldquo;more rude&rdquo; — was held to be &ldquo;a bald allegation and devoid of any material particulars.&rdquo; A fresh cause of action needs incidents, dates and specifics capable of proof.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No judge-made grounds.</strong> The court &ldquo;cannot grant a decree of divorce or of judicial separation on any grounds&rdquo; outside Sections 10 and 13. The power to dissolve a marriage for irretrievable breakdown &ldquo;is available only&rdquo; to the Supreme Court under its constitutional jurisdiction.</div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrine, mapped</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Maintainability</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Divorce dismissed; fresh divorce or judicial separation petition on the same pre-dismissal allegations</td><td>Barred — the earlier findings operate as res judicata between the spouses.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Fresh petition pleading specific, dated conduct occurring after the earlier dismissal</td><td>Maintainable — a new cause of action is the gateway, and it must be pleaded with particulars.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vague assertions of continued misbehaviour (&ldquo;became more rude&rdquo;)</td><td>Insufficient — adjectives without incidents do not constitute a cause of action.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Petition resting on irretrievable breakdown before a Family Court or High Court</td><td>Not available — breakdown is not a statutory ground; only the Supreme Court may dissolve on that basis.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Lessons for matrimonial practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Treat the first petition as the only petition: plead every incident with dates and particulars, because grounds omitted or rejected are lost for all later rounds.</p>
  <p>When advising a client after a failed divorce suit, audit what has happened <em>since</em> the dismissal — only that conduct can found a new case, and it must be documented as it occurs.</p>
  <p>Resist the temptation to re-label: shifting from Section 13 to Section 10 changes the relief, not the res judicata analysis.</p>
  <p>Where a marriage is truly dead but no statutory ground survives, the realistic routes are settlement and mutual consent — or, in exceptional cases, the Supreme Court&rsquo;s constitutional jurisdiction, which no lower forum can borrow.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">The judgment is a reminder that matrimonial litigation is cumulative: every pleading, finding and appeal becomes part of a single, continuing record of the marriage. Parties who litigate in instalments — saving allegations for the next round — find the courthouse doors progressively closing. The discipline the Bench demands is ultimately protective of both spouses: it forces the real case to be put, in full, the first time.</div>
